<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Silvius I Nimrod, Duke of Württemberg-Oels</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1622">1622</a>–<a href="/years/1664">1664</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Silvius I Nimrod (1622–1664) was a prince of the House of Württemberg who in 1647 married Elisabeth Marie, heiress of the last Poděbrady duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olesnica">Oleśnica</a>, and so became Duke of Oleśnica (Oels). He founded the Württemberg-Oels line, which ruled the Silesian duchy until it passed by marriage to the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> in the 18th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silvius-i-nimrod-duke-of-wurttemberg-oels.jpg" alt="Silvius I Nimrod, Duke of Württemberg-Oels" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silviuswuertt.-oels.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Silvius Nimrod was born on 2 May 1622 at Weiltingen, a son of Duke Julius Frederick of Württemberg-Weiltingen and Anna Sabina of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg. As a young man he served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>: in 1638 he was with the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernhard-of-saxe-weimar">Bernhard of Saxe-Weimar</a> during the siege of Breisach. On 1 May 1647 he married Elisabeth Marie, Duchess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olesnica">Oels</a> (1625–1686), at Oels. Her father, Duke Charles Frederick I, died a few weeks after the wedding.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Charles Frederick had been the last reigning duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olesnica">Oels</a>, so the duchy fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Crown of Bohemia</a> as a vacant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Ferdinand III</a> inherited it in his capacity as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-bohemia">King of Bohemia</a>. Only after lengthy negotiations was Silvius Nimrod invested with the duchy, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on 15 December 1648, in exchange for 20000 guilders and the Moravian lordship of Jevišovice. He then set about rebuilding a duchy that had suffered in the war, giving particular attention to education and the Church.</p><p>In 1652 he founded the Order of the Skull, which lasted until the 19th century. The poet Angelus Silesius served as his court physician from 1647 to 1652, and Matthäus Apelt had been his ducal councillor and director of court music until 1639.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Silvius Nimrod died of a stroke in April 1664 while visiting Brzezinka in Silesia. Under his will, Duke Christian of Liegnitz-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brzeg">Brieg</a> became guardian of his children, who were still minors. To prevent an imperial guardianship, which would probably have meant a Catholic upbringing, Christian sent the young princes to study at the Collegium illustre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>. The couple had five sons and two daughters, several of whom died young; Silvius II Frederick (1651–1697) and Christian Ulrich I (1652–1704) were among the sons who outlived their father.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Silvius I Nimrod of Württemberg-Oels” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After their line died out in 1492, the duchy went to the Bohemian Poděbrady family, who introduced Lutheranism, and in 1647 by marriage to <b>Sylvius Nimrod of Württemberg</b>, whose line made Oleśnica a small centre of Baroque court culture and Lutheran printing.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-olesnica">Duchy of Oleśnica</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Weiltingen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olesnica">Oleśnica</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.02,10.47&amp;year=1649" aria-label="Weiltingen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Silvius I Nimrod, Duke of Württemberg-Oels"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5584 5887h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5584 5887h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.02,10.47&amp;year=1649">What was here in 1649? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
